# Compiled binaries. `go build` in the repository root leaves one named after
# the module, and `git add -A` sweeps it in. Build to /tmp instead.
/smb
/cover.out
# Anything inside a command's directory that is not source. There is no cmd/
# here today -- see below -- and the rule stays anyway, because the moment one
# comes back is exactly the moment nobody is thinking about this file.
/cmd/*/*
!/cmd/*/*.go
!/cmd/*/*.hcl
!/cmd/*/*.md
!/cmd/*/go.mod
!/cmd/*/go.sum
!/cmd/*/testdata/

# The command that used to live in cmd/smb-server is now
# github.com/go-fileshare/fileshare, so the rules that named its binary went
# with it. The lesson they carried did not: ignore a directory by ALLOW-LIST,
# never by the name of the artefact you already met -- a 6.5 MB binary was
# committed here by the very change that renamed the command, because the new
# rule named the new name.

# ⛔ AND THE WAY THIS BIT US A THIRD TIME: the commit that retired the command
# deleted the ignore rules AND ran `git add -A` in one go. Deleting a rule
# UN-IGNORES whatever it was hiding -- the 10 MB binary was still sitting in
# the work tree -- so it went in as a new file. Deleting an ignore rule is a
# change to what `git add -A` will pick up; look at the WHOLE of `git status`
# before committing one, not the first few lines of it.
